  "text": "The horse was a key animal to the Celts. It served god, symbol, and everyday life. The Celts were known abroad for their riding, above all in war. The horse carried goods and marked the rank of a noble. Nobles drove in chariots. Lesser warriors formed the riding army. The dead too owned horses, for a noble was often buried with his horse and gear. The horse's link to the sun may explain this. The sun is seen to carry the dead to the west and bring them to rebirth at dawn. Celtic coins join horse and sun. The goddess Epona, whose name means horse, was the champion of the cavalry. Her image served the Roman army. The place of the horse shows in the taboo against eating horseflesh, a rule still felt in parts of Europe today. The taboo broke only at holy rites.\nIn Ireland the horse was linked to the goddess of Sovereignty. The banais\nrighe, the holy marriage of king and land goddess, was said to join the king\nwith a white mare. Some scholars doubt the claim. In other Indo-European lands\na similar rite is known. The goddess Macha proved herself swifter than the\nking's own team, and the race ended with her death. Irish folk said horses\ncould see the dead. A rider who looked between a horse's ears shared that\nsight. The true mare, called the fiorlar, was the seventh foal born to a dam in a row. No fairy could steal her. A healing plant called Mary's Clover, with curing power, grew where she was born.\nIn Wales the goddess Rhiannon rides a swift white horse in the first branch of\nthe Mabinogion, surrounded by singing birds. After her son was taken, she bore\nthe blame and carried every visitor to the palace on her back, a mark of the\nbond between the horse and the land. In England and Wales, horse figures are\ncut into chalk hillsides. Eleven white horses are carved there, and the White\nHorse of Uffington is the best known. The local people scour it clean of turf,\na task that has drawn thousands over the centuries. In the older burial of a\nhorse's head in a building's foundation, Celtic folk guarded the home.",
